'''''X-Men''''' is an American [[Superhero film|superhero]] [[film series]] based on the [[X-Men]], a group of characters created by [[Stan Lee]] and [[Jack Kirby]] for comic books published by [[Marvel Comics]]. Encompassing thirteen live-action films and two live-action television series, it was produced by [[20th Century Fox]]{{efn|From ''[[X-Men (film)|X-Men]]'' to ''[[Dark Phoenix (film)|Dark Phoenix]]'', the company was known as 20th Century Fox. After its rebranding in January 2020, ''[[The New Mutants (film)|The New Mutants]]'' would be the only film in the franchise released under the name [[20th Century Studios]].|name=20th}} and [[Marvel Entertainment]] from 2000 to 2020.

In the 1980s, Marvel embarked on efforts to adapt the X-Men stories to film. After prolonged development, Fox purchased rights in 1993 and developed a trilogy comprising ''[[X-Men (film)|X-Men]]'' (2000), ''[[X2 (film)|X2]]'' (2003), and ''[[X-Men: The Last Stand]]'' (2006). A succession of films followed, expanding the fictional universe. Plans for a reboot led to the production of four [[prequel]] films chronicling the adventures of the X-Men as young people, from ''[[X-Men: First Class]]'' (2011) to ''[[Dark Phoenix (film)|Dark Phoenix]]'' (2019). From 2009 to 2020, Fox oversaw the creation of another six films as ''X-Men'' [[spinoff (media)|spinoffs]], which feature [[Logan (film character)|Wolverine]], [[Wade Wilson (film character)|Deadpool]], and the [[New Mutants]]. The studio's television networks division produced ''[[Legion (TV series)|Legion]]'' and ''[[The Gifted (American TV series)|The Gifted]]'' (both 2017–2019) tangential to the films. Fox controlled the production of X-Men films until [[Acquisition of 21st Century Fox by Disney|its acquisition by The Walt Disney Company in 2019]], effectively terminating [[List of unproduced X-Men film series projects|all planned adaptations from the studio]].

Critical opinion about ''X-Men'' films differ significantly. Most average moderately positive scores on [[Metacritic]] and [[Rotten Tomatoes]], though there is a wide disparity between the best-reviewed entries, ''[[X-Men: Days of Future Past]]'' (2014) and ''[[Logan (film)|Logan]]'' (2017), and the worst, ''[[X-Men Origins: Wolverine]]'' (2009), ''Dark Phoenix'', and ''[[The New Mutants (film)|The New Mutants]]'' (2020). At the box office, the films are generally successful and constitute one of the [[List of highest-grossing films#Highest-grossing franchises and film series|highest-grossing film franchises]] of all time, grossing over $6{{nbsp}}billion worldwide. {{TOC limit|3}}

== Films ==

In the 1980s, [[Marvel Comics]] embarked on efforts to develop a [[theatrical film]] about the [[X-Men]], a superhero team of [[Mutant (Marvel Comics)|mutants]] created by [[Stan Lee]] and [[Jack Kirby]] in 1963, whose stories were the company's most popular to that point.{{sfnm|1a1=Knowles|1y=2007|1p=174|2a1=Tucker|2y=2017|2p=220}} Marvel [[option (filmmaking)|optioned]] the film rights to create a live-action adaptation with the Toronto-based animation studio [[Nelvana]].<ref name="polyxmen" /> [[Chris Claremont]] outlined two proposals between 1982 and 1983; his first outline gave particular focus to [[Kitty Pryde]], while the second pitted the X-Men against the [[Brotherhood of Mutants]] within the context of the [[Cold War]].<ref name="polyxmen">{{Cite web |url=https://www.polygon.com/2019/6/11/18659771/x-men-1980s-marvel-movie-orion-pictures/ |title=The abandoned X-Men movie of the 1980s isn't missed by anyone involved |first=Petrana |last=Radulovic |date=June 11, 2019 |website=[[Polygon (website)|Polygon]] |access-date=January 14, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251017142128/https://www.polygon.com/2019/6/11/18659771/x-men-1980s-marvel-movie-orion-pictures/ |archive-date=October 17, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Thereafter, Claremont left to concentrate on other endeavors. Around the time Nelvana finalized a distribution agreement with [[Orion Pictures]], [[Gerry Conway]] and [[Roy Thomas]] were hired to prepare a [[film treatment|treatment]].<ref name="polyxmen" /> The men wrote several treatments, owing to disputes with the Orion producers over the creative direction, triggering a prolonged development phase. Each draft increasingly deviated from the comics.<ref name="polyxmen" /> By 1985, Orion was in financial disarray after all but two films failed to yield profits, unravelling the project's development.<ref name="polyxmen" />

Toward the end of the decade, Marvel entered discussions with [[James Cameron]] to produce an X-Men film through a venture with his production company [[Lightstorm Entertainment]].{{sfn|Robinson|Gonzales|Edwards|2023}} Claremont and Lee pitched the idea to Lightstorm producers, but the negotiations collapsed as Cameron had obligations to another Marvel property, [[Spider-Man in film#Feature film development|Spider-Man]], with [[Carolco Pictures]] at the time.{{sfnm|1a1=Tucker|1y=2017|1p=221|2a1=Robinson|2a2=Gonzales|2a3=Edwards|2y=2023|2pp=}} Marvel spent another nine months soliciting bids for the sale of the X-Men film rights, ending when they arranged [[20th Century Fox]] to purchase said rights for $2.6 million in June 1993.<ref>{{cite web |last= |first= |title=Fox X's out bidding foes |website=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |date=June 29, 1993 |url=https://variety.com/1993/film/news/fox-x-s-out-bidding-foes-108279/ |access-date=January 15, 2026 |archive-date=January 15, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260115022521/https://variety.com/1993/film/news/fox-x-s-out-bidding-foes-108279/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Leston |first=Ryan |date=April 4, 2017 |title=How much did the X-Men cost 20th Century Fox? |url=https://uk.movies.yahoo.com/how-much-did-the-x-men-cost-20th-century-fox-154922092.html?guccounter=1&guce_referrer=aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZ29vZ2xlLmNvbS8&guce_referrer_sig=AQAAACFgr5LMZ0lY3MkyagNJZF22fz8ddU_JlNifqoACXxyC_b039ytz2pJ6sV6CIhJRQ73LtJ0mk8RaUNXZ9uVWIVQ2h1ATSctCVSjv6Ln478K-fpT3QvCkKXc3IJ7S8n3UsEByQpr4UiQaHsH88RNZmhiZMWw-B18giLS5JxSXD-bC |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170414092550/https://uk.movies.yahoo.com/how-much-did-the-x-men-cost-20th-century-fox-154922092.html |archive-date=April 14, 2017 |access-date=January 14, 2026 |publisher=[[Yahoo!]] |url-status=live}}</ref> The success of ''[[X-Men: The Animated Series]]'' (1992–1997) had piqued Hollywood interest in a feature film.<ref name="Origin">{{cite web |url=https://observer.com/2020/07/x-men-anniversary-fox-marvel-superhero-movies-kevin-feige/ |title=What 'X-Men' Pulled Off 20 Years Ago, According to Those Who Made It |first=Brandon |last=Katz |date=July 14, 2020 |work=[[The New York Observer]] |access-date=January 31, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250917165213/https://observer.com/2020/07/x-men-anniversary-fox-marvel-superhero-movies-kevin-feige/ |archive-date=September 17, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

