=== Original trilogy ===
==== ''X-Men'' (2000) ====
{{Main|X-Men (film)|l1=''X-Men'' (film)}}
[[File:Patrick Stewart Head Shot.jpg|thumb|upright|Patrick Stewart in 2009]]

Fox began work on ''X-Men'' with producer [[Lauren Shuler Donner]] in 1994.<ref name="xmen20001">{{Cite magazine |last=Siegel |first=Tatiana |date=July 31, 2020 |title=Bryan Singer's Traumatic 'X-Men' Set: The Movie "Created a Monster" |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/bryan-singers-traumatic-x-men-set-movie-created-a-monster-1305081/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251122081108/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/bryan-singers-traumatic-x-men-set-movie-created-a-monster-1305081/ |archive-date=November 22, 2025 |access-date=January 15, 2026 |magazine=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref> The studio approached [[Bryan Singer]] early in the film's development and, by July 1996, appointed him as director.<ref name="xmen20001" /> Singer was apprehensive about directing but was convinced by his producer [[Tom DeSanto]], an avid fan of the comics.{{sfn|Hughes|2007|p=209}} Singer was drawn to the X-Men stories because of their portrayal of the mutants' oppression.{{sfn|Hughes|2007|p=209}} Fox spent several years in protracted development over the creative direction, hiring a number of writers with the instruction to preserve the overtly political subtext espoused by the comics.{{sfn|Hughes|2007|p=210}} Filmmakers also wanted changes in the script to prioritize action scenes that could not be easily altered without raising the budget, which they were unwilling to do.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Seymour |first=Craig |date=May 10, 2000 |title=''Buffy'' creator slams ''The X-Men'' movie |url=https://ew.com/article/2000/05/10/buffy-creator-slams-x-men-movie/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170203200300/http://ew.com/article/2000/05/10/buffy-creator-slams-x-men-movie/ |archive-date=February 3, 2017 |access-date=May 22, 2007 |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]] |url-status=dead}}</ref> [[David Hayter]] received the sole screenwriting credit.{{sfn|Robinson|Gonzales|Edwards|2023}}

''X-Men''{{'s}} [[ensemble cast]] comprises twelve actors, including [[Hugh Jackman]], [[Patrick Stewart]], [[Ian McKellen]], [[Halle Berry]], [[Anna Paquin]], [[Famke Janssen]], and [[James Marsden]].<ref>{{Cite news |title='X-Men': Pow! Misfit Heroes to the Rescue! Zap! |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=July 14, 2000 |url=https://archive.nytimes.com/www.nytimes.com/library/film/071400xmen-film-review.html |access-date=January 15, 2026 |url-access= |first=Elvis |last=Mitchell |archive-date=August 26, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220826094501/https://archive.nytimes.com/www.nytimes.com/library/film/071400xmen-film-review.html |url-status=live}}</ref> The then-little known Jackman was not Fox's initial casting choice for [[Wolverine (character)|Wolverine]]; the studio contracted [[Dougray Scott]] in that role until an unforeseen scheduling conflict pertaining to delays in the production of ''[[Mission: Impossible 2]]'' (2000) forced the actor to drop out.{{sfnm|1a1=Cronin|1y=2018|1p=|2a1=Mell|2y=2015|2p=258}} ''X-Men'' follows Wolverine and [[Rogue (Marvel Comics)|Rogue]]'s (Paquin) entry into a global conflict between mutant factions led by [[Charles Xavier (film character)|Charles Xavier]] (Stewart) and [[Magneto (film character)|Magneto]], supervillain persona of Erik Lehnsherr (McKellen). The film, shot from September 1999 to March 2000 in Toronto, was released in July 2000 and became a surprise success.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schmitz |first=Greg Dean |author-link=Greg Dean Schmitz |date=May 30, 1999 |title=Greg's Preview – X-Men |url=https://movies.yahoo.com/movie/preview/1808406650 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070111234610/http://movies.yahoo.com/movie/preview/1808406650 |archive-date=January 11, 2007 |access-date=July 11, 2007 |publisher=[[Yahoo!]]}}</ref>{{sfn|Burke|2015|p=58}}

==== ''X2'' (2003) ====
{{Main|X2 (film)|l1=''X2'' (film)}}

A sequel to ''X-Men'' was in development by April 2001.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Fox, Marvel file 'X' suits |work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |date=April 15, 2001 |url=https://variety.com/2001/biz/news/fox-marvel-file-x-suits-1117797026/ |access-date=February 2, 2026 |url-access= |first=Tim |last=Swanson |archive-date=November 14, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211114013336/https://variety.com/2001/biz/news/fox-marvel-file-x-suits-1117797026/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Hayter and [[Zak Penn]] produced an early script combining elements of drafts they had written independently.<ref name="xcons">{{cite web |author=Stax |title=Lauren Shuler Donner Talks ''X-Men 2'' and ''Constantine'' |website=IGN |date=June 15, 2001 |url=http://movies.ign.com/articles/300/300581p1.html |access-date=February 2, 2026 |archive-date=September 29, 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090929083708/http://movies.ign.com/articles/300/300581p1.html |url-status=live}}</ref> Fox recruited [[Michael Dougherty]] and [[Dan Harris (screenwriter)|Dan Harris]] to make revisions to this screenplay; they submitted hundreds of drafts to Singer.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Grove |first=Martin A. |date=April 11, 2003 |title=Fox's 'X2' marks spot as presummer starts May 2 |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/hr/search/article_display.jsp?vnu_content_id=1863776 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070930201512/http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/hr/search/article_display.jsp?vnu_content_id=1863776 |archive-date=September 30, 2007 |access-date=April 15, 2007 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref> The ''X2'' story draws from Claremont's graphic novel ''[[X-Men: God Loves, Man Kills]]'' (1982).{{sfn|Smith|2013|p=840}} Shooting lasted five months in Vancouver, from June to November 2002.<ref name="greg">{{Cite web |last=Schmitz |first=Greg Dean |title=Greg's Preview – X2: X-Men United |url=https://movies.yahoo.com/movie/preview/1808406654 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070219003626/http://movies.yahoo.com/movie/preview/1808406654 |archive-date=February 19, 2007 |access-date=July 11, 2007 |publisher=Yahoo!}}</ref> ''X2'' was a trouble-ridden production, and problems were attributed to Singer's escalating dependence on drugs.<ref name="xmen20001" /> A confrontation arose when DeSanto urged a pause on filming in response to witnessing Singer inebriated, then another once Fox resumed production the following day, in which the actors threatened to quit.<ref name="xmen20001" /> ''X2'' debuted to mostly positive reviews in May 2003 and grossed $407 million at the box office, [[2003 in film#Highest-grossing films|finishing ninth among the year's highest-grossing films]].<ref name="x2mt" /><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.boxofficemojo.com/year/world/2003/?ref_=bo_cso_table_1 |title=2003 Worldwide Box Office |website=[[Box Office Mojo]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241127040051/https://www.boxofficemojo.com/year/world/2003/?ref_=bo_cso_table_1 |archive-date=November 27, 2024 |url-status=live |access-date=March 16, 2026}}</ref>

==== ''X-Men: The Last Stand'' (2006) ====
{{Main|X-Men: The Last Stand|l1=''X-Men: The Last Stand''}}

Singer was preparing a [[film treatment]] for a third film, ''X-Men: The Last Stand'', before resigning to direct ''[[Superman Returns]]'' (2006) in July 2004.<ref>{{cite web |last=Vespe, Eric (Quint) |title=Quint on Superman Returns gag reel & sequel talk with Singer |publisher=[[Ain't It Cool News]] |date=July 22, 2006 |url=https://www.aintitcool.com/node/23943 |access-date=February 4, 2026 |archive-date=February 10, 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090210142416/http://www.aintitcool.com/node/23943 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Dunkley |first1=Cathy |first2=Michael |last2=Fleming |name-list-style=amp |url=https://variety.com/2004/film/news/supe-s-on-with-x-man-1117907948/ |title=Supe's on with 'X' man |work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |date=July 18, 2004 |access-date=February 4, 2026 |archive-date=June 11, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190611222632/https://variety.com/2004/film/news/supe-s-on-with-x-man-1117907948/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Fox proceeded with the release of ''X-Men: The Last Stand'' without a director until they offered the role to [[Matthew Vaughn]], having been impressed with his work in the crime thriller ''[[Layer Cake (film)|Layer Cake]]'' (2004).<ref name="tax">{{cite news |first=Don |last=Townson |url=https://variety.com/2005/film/features/tax-credit-creation-1117918085/ |title=Tax credit creation |work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |date=February 16, 2005 |access-date=February 4, 2026 |archive-date=November 7, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121107062108/http://www.variety.com/article/VR1117918085 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Dave |last=McNary |url=https://variety.com/2005/film/markets-festivals/next-x-men-firms-up-1117919936/ |title=Next 'X-Men' firms up |work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |date=March 21, 2005 |access-date=February 4, 2026 |archive-date=November 7, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121107062233/http://www.variety.com/article/VR1117919936 |url-status=live}}</ref> Vaughn eventually dropped out and was replaced by [[Brett Ratner]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Fleming |first1=Michael |first2=Clause |last2=Brodesser |name-list-style=amp |url=https://variety.com/2005/film/markets-festivals/new-master-for-mutants-1117923934/ |title=New master for mutants |work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |date=June 5, 2005 |access-date=December 11, 2010 |archive-date=November 7, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121107062300/http://www.variety.com/article/VR1117923934 |url-status=live}}</ref> The screenplay was written by Vaughn, Penn, and [[Simon Kinberg]], though only the latter two received credit for their contributions.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.superherohype.com/features/167401-exclusive-interview-with-x-men-first-class-director-matthew-vaughn |title=Exclusive Interview with X-Men: First Class Director Matthew Vaughn |date=May 27, 2011 |access-date=July 31, 2022 |archive-date=July 31, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220731223744/https://www.superherohype.com/features/167401-exclusive-interview-with-x-men-first-class-director-matthew-vaughn |url-status=live}}</ref> They drew inspiration from the multi-issue storylines "[[The Dark Phoenix Saga]]" and "Gifted" for the film plot.<ref name="one">{{cite news |first=Sean |last=Elliot |url=http://www.ifmagazine.com/feature.asp?article=1525 |title=Exclusive Interview: Screenwriter Simon Kinberg Talks X-Men: The Last Stand – Part 1 |work=[[Electric Entertainment|iF Magazine]] |date=June 8, 2006 |access-date=February 4, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080617005244/http://www.ifmagazine.com/feature.asp?article=1525 |archive-date=June 17, 2008}}</ref> After a five month filming period, Fox released ''X-Men: The Last Stand'' in May 2006.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schmitz |first=Greg Dean |title=Greg's Preview – X-Men: The Last Stand |url=https://movies.yahoo.com/movie/preview/1808490829 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070110015704/http://movies.yahoo.com/movie/preview/1808490829 |archive-date=January 10, 2007 |access-date=August 3, 2007 |publisher=[[Yahoo!]]}}</ref><ref name="X3" /> It drew mixed reviews in the media and was 2006's [[2006 in film#Highest-grossing films|seventh highest-grossing film]].<ref name="laststandmt" /><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.boxofficemojo.com/year/world/2006/?ref_=bo_cso_table_1 |title=2006 Worldwide Box Office |website=[[Box Office Mojo]] |access-date=March 16, 2026}}</ref>

