The idea of a spinoff chronicling the adventures of the X-Men as young people was first articulated by Donner in 2003.<ref name="atom1">{{cite video |title="''Children of the Atom: Filming ''X-Men First Class" |type=Featurette |publisher=20th Century Fox Home Entertainment |location=''X-Men: First Class'' Blu-Ray |trans-title=Second Genesis}}</ref> Kinberg pitched a film version of the comic book series ''[[X-Men: First Class (comics)|X-Men: First Class]]'', which depicts the characters as teenagers.<ref name="atom1" /><ref name="wgacredit">{{Cite web |last1=Fernandez |first1=Jay A. |last2=Kit |first2=Borys |date=April 27, 2011 |title=Complex 'X-Men: First Class' Screenplay Credit Dispute Resolved (Exclusive) |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/complex-x-men-first-class-182356/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120426091742/http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/complex-x-men-first-class-182356 |archive-date=April 26, 2012 |access-date=May 6, 2012 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref> Fox envisioned ''X-Men: First Class'' as the first of a trilogy of films.<ref name="SingerProducingDeadline">{{cite news |date=March 26, 2010 |last=Fleming |first=Mike |title=Bryan Singer Producing ''X-Men: First Class'' |url=https://deadline.com/2010/03/singer-to-producex-men-first-class-29669/ |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]] |access-date=February 18, 2026 |archive-date=August 14, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130814082823/http://www.deadline.com/2010/03/singer-to-producex-men-first-class/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Its main story dwells on Xavier ([[James McAvoy]]) and Lehnsherr's ([[Michael Fassbender]]) relationship as twenty-somethings and the point at which their friendship dissolves.<ref name="wgacredit" /><ref name="Boucher">{{cite news |first=Geoff |last=Boucher |url=http://latimesblogs.latimes.com/herocomplex/2010/03/bryan-singer-and-the-xmen-together-again.html |title=Bryan Singer on ''X-Men: First Class'': It's got to be about Magneto and Professor X |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |date=March 18, 2010 |access-date=February 18, 2026 |archive-date=March 22, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100322191808/http://latimesblogs.latimes.com/herocomplex/2010/03/bryan-singer-and-the-xmen-together-again.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

The script went through multiple revisions from a succession of writers and incorporated ideas gleaned from an abandoned Magneto spinoff written by [[Sheldon Turner]] under [[David Goyer]]'s direction, igniting a dispute over the distribution of screenwriting credits.<ref name="wgacredit" /> In response, a [[Writers Guild of America, West|Writers Guild of America]] (WGA) [[WGA screenwriting credit system|arbitration]] credited Turner and Singer, who had supervised an early script by [[Jamie Moss]], as the story's creators.<ref name="wgacredit" /> The WGA upheld their ruling when Moss [[appeal (law)|appealed]].<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Kits |first1=Borys |date=April 29, 2011 |title='X-Men: First Class' Writing Credits Appeal Denied |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/x-men-first-class-writing-183768/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260205225436/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/x-men-first-class-writing-183768/ |archive-date=February 5, 2026 |access-date=February 5, 2026 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref> Singer remained involved in ''X-Men: First Class'' as a producer.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Fleming |first=Mike |date=March 26, 2010 |title=Bryan Singer Producing 'X-Men: First Class' |url=https://deadline.com/2010/03/singer-to-producex-men-first-class-29669/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130814082823/http://www.deadline.com/2010/03/singer-to-producex-men-first-class/ |archive-date=August 14, 2013 |access-date=July 9, 2010 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref> Fox hired Vaughn as the film's director in May 2010.<ref>{{cite news |last=Fleming |first=Mike |title=Fox Signs Vaughn To Direct 'X-Men: First Class' |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]] |date=May 4, 2010 |url=https://deadline.com/2010/05/fox-seals-deal-for-matthew-vaughn-to-direct-x-men-first-class-38098/ |access-date=February 5, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250810155437/https://deadline.com/2010/05/fox-seals-deal-for-matthew-vaughn-to-direct-x-men-first-class-38098/ |archive-date=August 10, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> McAvoy and Fassbender lead a fourteen-actor ensemble cast featuring, among others, [[Jennifer Lawrence]], [[Kevin Bacon]], [[Rose Byrne]], [[January Jones]], and [[Nicholas Hoult]].<ref>{{Cite news |title=Born That Way, and Proud of It |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=June 2, 2011 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/movies/x-men-first-class-review.html |access-date=February 4, 2026 |url-access=subscription |first=Manohla |last=Dargis |archive-date=April 3, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220403180629/https://www.nytimes.com/2011/06/03/movies/x-men-first-class-review.html |url-status=live}}</ref> The filmmakers shot ''X-Men: First Class'' into early 2011.<ref name="ves">{{cite web |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/fox-exec-at-ves-panel-242957 |title=Fox Exec at VES Panel: We Thought James Franco's Character Should Die in 'Apes' |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |date=October 2, 2011 |access-date=February 5, 2026 |first=Carolyn |last=Giardina |archive-date=January 4, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120104200336/http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/fox-exec-at-ves-panel-242957 |url-status=live}}</ref> Despite a poor box office prognosis,<ref>{{cite news |url=http://today.msnbc.msn.com/id/43286389/ns/today-entertainment/ |title='X-Men' mutants weaken with prequel debut |publisher=[[MSNBC]] |date=June 5, 2011 |access-date=February 9, 2026 |author=Germain, David |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110608132118/http://today.msnbc.msn.com/id/43286389/ns/today-entertainment/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=June 8, 2011}}</ref> the film opened to positive reviews in June 2011, finishing the theatrical run with $353 million.<ref name="fcmt" /><ref name="Xfc" />

==== ''X-Men: Days of Future Past'' (2014) ====
{{Main|X-Men: Days of Future Past|l1=''X-Men: Days of Future Past''}}

Donner confirmed plans for a sequel, ''X-Men: Days of Future Past'', in a March 2011 cover story published by ''[[Empire (magazine)|Empire]]''.<ref>{{cite web |first=Darren |last=Franich |url=https://ew.com/article/2011/03/29/x-men-deadpool-wolverine-x4-x5/ |title='X-Men' movie round-up: Producer teases upcoming 'Deadpool,' 'Wolverine,' 'X4,' and 'X5' projects |date=March 29, 2011 |work=[[Entertainment Weekly]] |access-date=February 18, 2026 |archive-date=December 28, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231228004259/https://ew.com/article/2011/03/29/x-men-deadpool-wolverine-x4-x5/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Kinberg returned and became the franchise's primary writer.<ref>{{cite web |first=Owen |last=Williams |url=https://www.empireonline.com/movies/news/x-men-first-class-sequel-writer/ |title=X-Men: First Class Sequel Has A Writer |date=November 4, 2011 |work=[[Empire (magazine)|Empire]] |access-date=February 18, 2026 |archive-date=April 23, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250423182410/https://www.empireonline.com/movies/news/x-men-first-class-sequel-writer/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="primarywriter">{{cite web |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/foxs-x-men-issues-jennifer-lawrence-unsigned-deadpool-defections-gambit-hold-945505/ |title=Fox's X-Men Issues: Jennifer Lawrence Unsigned, 'Deadpool' Defections, 'Gambit' on Hold |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |date=November 9, 2016 |access-date=February 19, 2026 |first=Borys |last=Kit |archive-date=December 9, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251209161147/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/foxs-x-men-issues-jennifer-lawrence-unsigned-deadpool-defections-gambit-hold-945505/ |url-status=live}}</ref> The producers originally hired Vaughn to direct; Singer went into the project as Vaughn's replacement after he left to focus on the creation of ''[[Kingsman: The Secret Service]]'' (2014).<ref>{{Cite web |last=Trumbore |first=Dave |date=October 25, 2012 |title=Matthew Vaughn Out as Director of 'X-Men: First Class' Sequel, 'X-Men: Days Of Future Past'; Bryan Singer May Replace Him |url=https://collider.com/bryan-singer-x-men-first-class-2-sequel/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131029195056/http://collider.com/bryan-singer-x-men-first-class-2-sequel/206289/ |archive-date=October 29, 2013 |access-date=May 27, 2017 |website=[[Collider (website)|Collider]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Vary |first=Adam B. |date=October 30, 2012 |title=Bryan Singer will direct 'X-Men: Days of Future Past' |url=http://insidemovies.ew.com/2012/10/30/bryan-singer-directing-x-men-days-of-future-past/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121031233708/http://insidemovies.ew.com/2012/10/30/bryan-singer-directing-x-men-days-of-future-past/ |archive-date=October 31, 2012 |access-date=October 31, 2012 |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]]}}</ref> Kinberg's script borrows from the "[[Days of Future Past]]" storyline though features Wolverine time traveling instead of Kitty Pryde ([[Elliot Page]]).<ref name="Collider set visit">{{cite web |url=https://collider.com/x-men-days-of-future-past-set-visit/ |title=X-Men: Days of Future Past Set Visit: 90 Thing to Know About X-Men: Days of Future Past |first=Steve |last=Weintruab |website=[[Collider (website)|Collider]] |date=February 19, 2014 |access-date=February 18, 2026 |archive-date=September 1, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160901123724/http://collider.com/x-men-days-of-future-past-set-visit/ |url-status=live}}</ref> The filmmakers also used time travel to alter the continuity of events established in ''X-Men: The Last Stand''.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/x-men-apocalypse-confusing-chronology-897957/ |title='X-Men: Apocalypse': The Confusing Chronology Behind the Movie's Cameo Appearance |last=McMillan |first=Graeme |date=May 27, 2016 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |access-date=February 19, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260114025742/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/x-men-apocalypse-confusing-chronology-897957/ |archive-date=January 14, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |first=Darren |last=Franich |url=https://ew.com/article/2014/05/23/x-men-ending-future-past/ |title='X-Men: Days of Future Past' ending: What it means |date=May 23, 2014 |work=[[Entertainment Weekly]] |access-date=February 18, 2026 |archive-date=July 26, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230726005719/https://ew.com/article/2014/05/23/x-men-ending-future-past/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Fox gave the production a $205 million budget, spending mid-2013 filming ''X-Men: Days of Future Past'' in Montreal,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.slashfilm.com/529105/is-x-men-days-of-future-past-foxs-second-most-expensive-movie-ever/ |title=Is 'X-Men: Days Of Future Past' Fox's Second Most Expensive Movie Ever? |first=Angie |last=Han |date=November 20, 2013 |work=[[SlashFilm|/Film]] |access-date=February 18, 2026 |archive-date=October 10, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251010000734/https://www.slashfilm.com/529105/is-x-men-days-of-future-past-foxs-second-most-expensive-movie-ever/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nme.com/news/film/shooting-has-wrapped-on-x-men-days-of-future-past-874956 |title=Shooting has wrapped on 'X-Men: Days Of Future Past', director confirms |first=Nick |last=Levine |date=August 19, 2013 |work=[[NME]] |access-date=February 18, 2026 |archive-date=August 17, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190817213533/https://www.nme.com/news/film/shooting-has-wrapped-on-x-men-days-of-future-past-874956 |url-status=live}}</ref> with reshoots commencing toward the end of the year.<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 17, 2013 |title=X-Men returns to Montreal for reshoots |url=https://www.calgaryherald.com/entertainment/movie-guide/returns%2BMontreal%2Breshoots/9177608/story.html |first=Brendan |last=Kelly |work=[[Calgary Herald]] |access-date=February 18, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131121034330/https://www.calgaryherald.com/entertainment/movie-guide/returns+Montreal+reshoots/9177608/story.html |archive-date=November 21, 2013 |url-status=dead}}</ref> Filming was affected by Singer's frequent absences, forcing Kinberg to manage daily operations with Hutch Parker, [[Newton Thomas Sigel]], and [[John Ottman]].<ref name="awol">{{cite news |url=https://deadline.com/2019/06/dark-phoenix-bombs-at-the-box-office-reasons-why-1202629749/ |title='Dark Phoenix' Bound To Lose $100M+ After Worst Domestic Opening In 'X-Men' Series: Here's Why |last=D'Alessandro |first=Anthony |work=[[Deadline Hollywood]] |date=June 9, 2019 |access-date=February 20, 2026 |archive-date=November 24, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251124081521/https://deadline.com/2019/06/dark-phoenix-bombs-at-the-box-office-reasons-why-1202629749/ |url-status=live}}</ref> The theatrical rollout followed in May 2014.<ref name="Xdofp" /> ''X-Men: Days of Future Past'' emerged as the [[2014 in film#Highest-grossing films|sixth highest-grossing film of 2014]] with $746 million.<ref name="profit">{{cite news |url=https://deadline.com/2015/03/x-men-days-of-future-past-profit-box-office-2014-1201389620/ |title=No. 16 ''X-Men: Days Of Future Past'' – 2014 Most Valuable Blockbuster Movie Tournament |last=Fleming |first=Mike Jr. |work=[[Deadline Hollywood]] |date=March 10, 2015 |access-date=February 18, 2026 |archive-date=June 30, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230630221420/https://deadline.com/2015/03/x-men-days-of-future-past-profit-box-office-2014-1201389620/ |url-status=live}}</ref> At the [[87th Academy Awards]], the film was nominated for [[Academy Award for Best Visual Effects|Best Visual Effects]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=February 22, 2015 |title=Oscars 2015: The Winners List |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/oscars-2015-winners-list-776233/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220510092242/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/oscars-2015-winners-list-776233/ |archive-date=May 10, 2022 |access-date=March 16, 2026 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref>

==== ''X-Men: Apocalypse'' (2016) ====
{{Main|X-Men: Apocalypse|l1=''X-Men: Apocalypse''}}
[[File:Sophie Turner (40553144273) (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright|Sophie Turner in 2019]]

Preparation for the follow-up film, titled ''X-Men: Apocalypse'', began months before ''X-Men: Days of Future Past''{{'s}} 2014 release.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/release-date-announced-x-men-663154 |title=Release date announced for X-Men: Apocalypse |last1=Ford |first1=Rebecca |first2=Borys |last2=Kit |date=December 5, 2013 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |access-date=February 19, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141027111510/http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/release-date-announced-x-men-663154 |archive-date=October 27, 2014 |url-status=live}}</ref> Singer was rehired as director, while Fox assigned the responsibility for scriptwriting to Kinberg, Dougherty, and Harris.<ref>{{cite web |last=Fleming |first=Mike Jr. |title=Bryan Singer set to helm X-Men: Apocalypse |url=https://deadline.com/2014/09/bryan-singer-xmen-apocalypse-director-sequel-838588/ |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]] |access-date=February 19, 2026 |date=September 22, 2014 |archive-date=February 17, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150217053120/http://deadline.com/2014/09/bryan-singer-xmen-apocalypse-director-sequel-838588/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Scott |last=Collura |title=X2 writers working on X-Men: Apocalypse |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2013/12/19/x2-writers-working-on-x-men-apocalypse |website=[[IGN]] |date=December 19, 2013 |access-date=February 19, 2026 |archive-date=July 25, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180725111234/http://www.ign.com/articles/2013/12/19/x2-writers-working-on-x-men-apocalypse |url-status=live}}</ref> For ''X-Men: Apocalypse'', the filmmakers conceived an X-Men origin story inspired by the 1988 story arc "[[The Fall of the Mutants]]", introducing [[Jean Grey]] ([[Sophie Turner]]), [[Cyclops (Marvel Comics)|Cyclops]] ([[Tye Sheridan]]), [[Storm (Marvel Comics)|Storm]] ([[Alexandra Shipp]]), and [[Apocalypse (Marvel Comics)|Apocalypse]] ([[Oscar Isaac]]).<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Stack |first=Tim |date=January 7, 2014 |title=X-Men: Apocalypse: Bryan Singer teases new film 'will address mutant origins' — EXCLUSIVE |url=http://insidemovies.ew.com/2014/01/07/x-men-apocalypse-bryan-singer/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150128043744/http://insidemovies.ew.com/2014/01/07/x-men-apocalypse-bryan-singer/ |archive-date=January 28, 2015 |access-date=May 26, 2017 |magazine=Entertainment Weekly}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://screenrant.com/x-men-apocalypse-magneto-powers-michael-fassbender/ |title=X-Men Apocalypse: Why has Magneto left his powers behind? |first=Bob |last=Chipman |website=[[Screen Rant]] |date=January 23, 2016 |access-date=February 19, 2026 |archive-date=October 6, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161006151509/http://screenrant.com/x-men-apocalypse-magneto-powers-michael-fassbender/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=White |first=James |date=April 29, 2015 |title=Exclusive: Bryan Singer reveals new X-Men: Apocalypse concept art |url=https://www.empireonline.com/news/story.asp?NID=44152 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170815000542/http://www.empireonline.com/movies/news/exclusive-bryan-singer-reveals-new-x-men-apocalypse-concept-art/ |archive-date=August 15, 2017 |access-date=February 19, 2026 |website=[[Empire (magazine)|Empire]]}}</ref> As well, they wanted resolution for narratives about Xavier, Lehnsherr, [[Mystique (Marvel Comics)|Mystique]] (Lawrence), and [[Beast (Marvel Comics)|Beast]] (Hoult) that began in ''X-Men: First Class''.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://collider.com/x-men-apocalypse-culmination-of-trilogy-says-simon-kinberg/ |title=X-Men: Apocalypse is the "culmination" of a trilogy, says Simon Kinberg |website=[[Collider (website)|Collider]] |date=September 21, 2015 |access-date=February 19, 2026 |archive-date=April 28, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210428220609/https://collider.com/x-men-apocalypse-culmination-of-trilogy-says-simon-kinberg/ |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Principal photography]] lasted from April to August 2015,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schaefer |first=Sandy |date=April 27, 2015 |title='X-Men: Apocalypse' Begins Production; First Official Look at Kodi Smit-McPhee as Nightcrawler |url=https://screenrant.com/x-men-apocalypse-filming-nightcrawler-actor-kodi-smit-mcphree-images/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150429143244/http://screenrant.com/x-men-apocalypse-filming-nightcrawler-actor-kodi-smit-mcphree-images/ |archive-date=April 29, 2015 |access-date=April 27, 2015 |website=[[Screen Rant]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Jayson |first=Jay |date=August 22, 2015 |title=X-Men: Apocalypse Wraps Main Filming |url=https://comicbook.com/2015/08/23/x-men-apocalypse-wraps-main-filming/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150823061803/http://comicbook.com/2015/08/23/x-men-apocalypse-wraps-main-filming/ |archive-date=August 23, 2015 |access-date=August 22, 2015 |website=[[ComicBook.com]]}}</ref> and ''X-Men: Apocalypse'' opened in May 2016 to mixed reviews and a diminished box office take of $543.9 million.<ref name="apocmt" /><ref name="Xa" /> After the film's release, Singer came under public scrutiny from reports of his conduct as early as 1997, including allegations of rape.<ref name="xmen20001" /><ref name="awol2">{{cite web |last=Sharf |first=Zack |date=June 10, 2019 |title=Before the 'Bohemian Rhapsody' Mess, Bryan Singer Reportedly Went AWOL From 'X-Men' Movie Sets |url=https://www.indiewire.com/features/general/bryan-singer-missing-xmen-sets-bohemian-rhapsody-1202148666/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250825165640/https://www.indiewire.com/features/general/bryan-singer-missing-xmen-sets-bohemian-rhapsody-1202148666/ |archive-date=August 25, 2025 |access-date=February 20, 2026 |website=[[IndieWire]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/general-news/why-fox-hired-fired-bryan-singer-bohemian-rhapsody-1156527/ |title="How Many At-Bats Do You Get?": Why Fox Hired (Then Fired) Bryan Singer on 'Bohemian Rhapsody' |last=Masters |first=Kim |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |date=October 31, 2018 |access-date=February 20, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251126184819/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/news/general-news/why-fox-hired-fired-bryan-singer-bohemian-rhapsody-1156527/ |archive-date=November 26, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

==== ''Dark Phoenix'' (2019) ====
{{Main|Dark Phoenix (film)|l1=''Dark Phoenix''}}

Producers had been contemplating a film adapting "The Dark Phoenix Saga" as early as 2003.<ref name="PennX2">{{cite news |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/ready-player-one-screenwriter-zak-penn-his-best-worst-movies-1097883 |title=''Ready Player One'' Screenwriter Zak Penn Looks Back at His Hits (and Famous Early Miss) |last=Couch |first=Aaron |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |date=March 29, 2018 |access-date=March 30, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180330104207/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/ready-player-one-screenwriter-zak-penn-his-best-worst-movies-1097883 |archive-date=March 30, 2018 |url-status=live}}</ref> Largely based on Fox's wishes, they developed the first film version of the story as a subplot for ''X-Men: The Last Stand'', but this treatment was poorly received.<ref name="darkphoenix1">{{cite news |url=https://time.com/5601749/dark-phoenix-x-men-the-last-stand-comparison/ |title=Dark Phoenix Tries to Correct the Errors of X-Men: The Last Stand. Does It Succeed? |last=Dockterman |first=Eliana |work=[[Time (magazine)|Time]] |date=June 7, 2019 |access-date=February 20, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251019015725/https://time.com/5601749/dark-phoenix-x-men-the-last-stand-comparison/ |archive-date=October 19, 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Stack |first=Tim |date=December 7, 2017 |title=X-Men: Dark Phoenix heats up EW's First Look Issue |url=https://ew.com/movies/2017/12/07/xmen-dark-phoenix-first-look-cover |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180103095330/http://ew.com/movies/2017/12/07/xmen-dark-phoenix-first-look-cover/ |archive-date=January 3, 2018 |access-date=August 24, 2018 |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]]}}</ref> Kinberg started writing a script for ''Dark Phoenix'' in 2016.<ref name="primarywriter" /> Fox then ousted Singer for Kinberg as director,<ref name="awol" /><ref name="awol2" /> news of which was confirmed in the media in June 2017.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Fleming, Jr. |first=Ian |date=June 14, 2017 |title=Fox Formalizes Simon Kinberg To Helm 'X-Men: Dark Phoenix'; Jennifer Lawrence, Michael Fassbender, James McAvoy Back, Jessica Chastain In Talks |url=https://deadline.com/2017/06/x-men-dark-phoenix-jennifer-lawrence-michael-fassbender-james-mcavoy-simon-kinberg-1202113290/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251221121328/https://deadline.com/2017/06/x-men-dark-phoenix-jennifer-lawrence-michael-fassbender-james-mcavoy-simon-kinberg-1202113290/ |archive-date=December 21, 2025 |access-date=February 20, 2026 |work=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref> The script portrays an origin story detailing Jean Grey's transformation into the [[Phoenix Force|Phoenix]], with emphasis on her mental health struggles.<ref name="auto">{{cite web |title=Dark Phoenix's VFX Supervisor Opens Up About the Astonishing Jean Grey That Could Have Been |url=https://io9.gizmodo.com/dark-phoenixs-vfx-supervisor-opens-up-about-the-astonis-1836850921 |website=[[io9]] |date=July 31, 2019 |access-date=February 17, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200430114153/https://io9.gizmodo.com/dark-phoenixs-vfx-supervisor-opens-up-about-the-astonis-1836850921 |archive-date=April 30, 2020 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.glamour.com/story/dark-phoenix-review |title=There's More to Sophie Turner's Role in 'Dark Phoenix' Than You Think |last=Moeslein |first=Anna |date=June 7, 2019 |work=[[Glamour (magazine)|Glamour]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230812225358/https://www.glamour.com/story/dark-phoenix-review |archive-date=August 12, 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref> ''Dark Phoenix'' had been imagined as a [[List of films split into multiple parts|two-part film]]; Fox insisted on a single film near the end of pre-production.<ref name="awol" /> Shooting occurred from June to October 2017, but reshoots extended the production schedule by a year because the actors had other work commitments.<ref name="debacle">{{Cite web |last=Kit |first=Borys |date=June 10, 2019 |title="We Were Wrong": Behind Fox's 'Dark Phoenix' Debacle |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/we-were-wrong-behind-dark-phoenix-foxs-dismal-x-men-franchise-finale-plans-1216859/ |access-date=February 20, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260205023044/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/we-were-wrong-behind-dark-phoenix-foxs-dismal-x-men-franchise-finale-plans-1216859/ |archive-date=February 5, 2026 |url-status=live |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref> Thus, the studio for a time postponed the exhibition schedule before releasing the film in June 2019.<ref name="debacle" /> ''Dark Phoenix'' was ridiculed by critics and brought Fox a $133 million profit loss, becoming one of the [[List of biggest box-office bombs|biggest box-office bombs of all time]].<ref name="debacle" /><ref name="DeadlineLosses">{{cite web |url=https://deadline.com/2020/04/box-office-bombs-2019-list-1202918753/ |title=The Biggest Box Office Bombs Of 2019: Deadline's Most Valuable Blockbuster Tournament |first=Anthony |last=D'Alessandro |work=[[Deadline Hollywood]] |date=April 27, 2020 |access-date=February 20, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200428012104/https://deadline.com/2020/04/box-office-bombs-2019-list-1202918753/ |archive-date=April 28, 2020 |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== ''Wolverine'' trilogy ===
==== ''X-Men Origins: Wolverine'' (2009) ====
{{Main|X-Men Origins: Wolverine|l1=''X-Men Origins: Wolverine''}}
[[File:Hugh Jackman - SDCC13.jpg|upright|thumb|Hugh Jackman in 2013]]

By October 2004, Fox initiated plans for a spinoff exploring Wolverine's origins.<ref name="xmarks" /> The studio hired [[David Benioff]] to write the ''X-Men Origins: Wolverine'' screenplay,<ref name="xmarks">{{Cite web |last=Fleming |first=Michael |date=October 4, 2004 |title='X' marks spinoff spot |url=https://variety.com/2004/film/markets-festivals/x-marks-spinoff-spot-1117911455/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060104172506/http://www.variety.com/article/VR1117911455?categoryid=13&cs=1 |archive-date=January 4, 2006 |access-date=September 1, 2006 |website=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]}}</ref> which assembled from a rewrite by [[Skip Woods]] and uncredited contributions from [[David Ayer]], [[James Vanderbilt]], and [[Scott Silver]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://filmbuffonline.com/ReadingRoom/Wolverine.htm |title=''X-Men Origins: Wolverine'' Script Review |publisher=FilmBuffOnline |access-date=February 23, 2026 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090120091636/http://filmbuffonline.com/ReadingRoom/Wolverine.htm |archive-date=January 20, 2009 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://movies.ign.com/articles/823/823830p1.html |title=New Wolverine Screenwriter |author=Stax |publisher=[[IGN]] |date=September 28, 2007 |access-date=February 23, 2026 |archive-date=February 18, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120218231409/http://movies.ign.com/articles/823/823830p1.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |author1=Michael Fleming |author2=Pamela McClintock |url=https://variety.com/2007/biz/markets-festivals/studios-prep-backup-plan-1117975064/ |title=Studios prep back-up plan |work=Variety |date=October 30, 2007 |access-date=February 23, 2026 |archive-date=November 11, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191111203417/https://variety.com/2007/biz/markets-festivals/studios-prep-backup-plan-1117975064/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Several filmmakers were considered to direct, which Fox eventually assigned to [[Gavin Hood]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.inverse.com/entertainment/x-men-origins-wolverine-15-year-anniversary |title=One of the worst X-Men movies made way for the best |work=[[Inverse (website)|Inverse]] |first=Ryan |last=Scott |date=May 1, 2024 |access-date=March 16, 2026 |archive-date=January 17, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260117011744/https://www.inverse.com/entertainment/x-men-origins-wolverine-15-year-anniversary |url-status=live}}</ref> Jackman starred and co-produced ''Wolverine'' through his production company [[Seed Productions]], earning $20 million.<ref name="seed">{{Cite web |last=Fleming |first=Michael |date=May 4, 2009 |title=Hugh Jackman, Seeds planting pics |url=https://variety.com/2009/film/markets-festivals/hugh-jackman-seeds-planting-pics-1118003176/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241127055617/https://variety.com/2009/film/markets-festivals/hugh-jackman-seeds-planting-pics-1118003176/ |archive-date=November 27, 2024 |access-date=February 23, 2026 |website=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]}}</ref><ref name="hunt">{{cite news |last=Galloway |first=Steven |title=Studios Are Hunting the Next Big Property |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |date=July 10, 2007 |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/hr/content_display/film/news/e3if727c623f03c782b8ad564866c828796 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071012001630/http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/hr/content_display/film/news/e3if727c623f03c782b8ad564866c828796 |archive-date=October 12, 2007 |access-date=January 16, 2010}}</ref> The film was shot in New Zealand and Australia beginning in January 2008 and finished in May.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/queenstown-gets-ready-for-x-men/X76JT6ZYL4IIVRHTW3QHUM6OMQ/ |title=Queenstown gets ready for X-Men |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |first=Jarrod |last=Booker |date=January 24, 2008 |access-date=March 16, 2026 |archive-date=April 16, 2025 |url-access=subscription |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250416011316/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/queenstown-gets-ready-for-x-men/X76JT6ZYL4IIVRHTW3QHUM6OMQ/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |author=Bentley, David |title=Filming wraps on Wolverine as Jackman offers chance to meet him for lunch |work=[[Coventry Telegraph]] |date=May 29, 2008 |url=http://blogs.coventrytelegraph.net/thegeekfiles/2008/05/post.html |access-date=March 16, 2026 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120629050118/http://blogs.coventrytelegraph.net/thegeekfiles/2008/05/post.html |archive-date=June 29, 2012 }}</ref> Critics expressed little enthusiasm upon the film's general release in May 2009 but, having grossed $373 million, ''Wolverine'' proved just successful enough to turn a profit.<ref name="originsmt" /><ref name="Xow" /><ref>{{Cite news |title=How X-Men Origins: Wolverine Paved the Way for Logan by Being So Terrible |work=[[Vanity Fair (magazine)|Vanity Fair]] |date=March 7, 2017 |url=https://www.vanityfair.com/hollywood/2017/03/logan-x-men-origins-wolverine-hugh-jackman-patrick-stewart-deadpool |access-date=February 27, 2026 |url-access=subscription |first=Nathan |last=Rabin |archive-date=February 4, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260204065038/https://www.vanityfair.com/hollywood/2017/03/logan-x-men-origins-wolverine-hugh-jackman-patrick-stewart-deadpool |url-status=live}}</ref>

==== ''The Wolverine'' (2013) ====
{{Main|The Wolverine (film)|l1=''The Wolverine'' (film)}}

A sequel, ''The Wolverine'', was commissioned immediately after ''X-Men Origins: Wolverine''{{'s}} release.<ref name="seed" /> Written by [[Scott Frank]] and [[Mark Bomback]],<ref>{{cite news |url=https://deadline.com/2023/12/scott-frank-script-doctor-saving-private-ryan-1235684157/ |title=Scott Frank Discusses The Films He's Worked On As A Script Doctor, Including 'Saving Private Ryan,' 'Gravity' And "A Lot Of The X-Men Movies" |last=Tapp |first=Tom |work=[[Deadline Hollywood]] |date=December 31, 2023 |access-date=February 23, 2026 |archive-date=July 24, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250724121525/https://deadline.com/2023/12/scott-frank-script-doctor-saving-private-ryan-1235684157/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Sneider |first=Jeff |date=September 1, 2011 |title='Total Recall' scribe rewriting 'Wolverine' |url=https://variety.com/2011/film/news/total-recall-scribe-rewriting-wolverine-1118042116/ |access-date=September 2, 2011 |work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]}}</ref> the film sees Wolverine dispatched to Japan to protect [[Mariko Yashida]] ([[Tao Okamoto]]), heiress of Yashida Industries, from [[yakuza]] assassins deployed by her father [[Shingen Yashida|Shingen]] ([[Hiroyuki Sanada]]), in a story based on the four-issue miniseries by Claremont and [[Frank Miller]], ''[[Wolverine (comic book)|Wolverine]]'' (1982).<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hewitt |first=Chris |date=October 25, 2012 |title=James Mangold Talks The Wolverine |url=https://www.empireonline.com/news/story.asp?NID=35599 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121113134358/http://www.empireonline.com/news/story.asp?NID=35599 |archive-date=November 13, 2012 |access-date=December 2, 2018 |work=[[Empire (magazine)|Empire]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Carter |first=Justin |date=July 26, 2023 |title=The Wolverine Was the Reliable Soldier Fox's X-Men Needed |url=https://gizmodo.com/the-wolverine-x-men-movie-10-year-retrospective-1850633051 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230726203733/https://gizmodo.com/the-wolverine-x-men-movie-10-year-retrospective-1850633051 |archive-date=July 26, 2023 |access-date=February 23, 2026 |website=[[Gizmodo]]}}</ref> Fox chose [[James Mangold]] as ''The Wolverine''{{'s}} director from a shortlist of eight filmmakers.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Fleming |first=Mike |date=June 16, 2011 |title=Fox Chooses James Mangold On 'Wolverine' |url=https://deadline.com/2011/06/james-mangold-foxs-choice-for-the-wolverine-140349/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110617033256/http://www.deadline.com/2011/06/james-mangold-foxs-choice-for-the-wolverine/ |archive-date=June 17, 2011 |access-date=June 16, 2011 |work=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref><ref name="Abrams">{{cite news |last=Abrams |first=Rachel |date=May 25, 2011 |title=Fox's 'Wolverine' helmer search down to 8 |work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |url=https://variety.com/2011/film/news/fox-s-wolverine-helmer-search-down-to-8-1118037600/ |access-date=May 26, 2011}}</ref> Production took place in Australia from August to November 2012,<ref name="dailytelegraph">{{cite news |date=July 31, 2012 |url=http://www.dailytelegraph.com.au/entertainment/movies/hugh-jackman-pumped-on-day-one-of-filming-the-wolverine/story-e6frexli-1226439137342 |title=Hugh Jackman 'Pumped' on Day one of 'Wolverine' Filming |work=[[The Daily Telegraph (Sydney)|The Daily Telegraph]] |access-date=March 16, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120731231435/http://www.dailytelegraph.com.au/entertainment/movies/hugh-jackman-pumped-on-day-one-of-filming-the-wolverine/story-e6frexli-1226439137342 |archive-date=July 31, 2012 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="coventrytelegraph2">{{cite news |date=November 22, 2012 |last=Bentley |first=David |url=http://blogs.coventrytelegraph.net/thegeekfiles/2012/11/filming-wraps-on-the-wolverine-first-trailer-due-early-2013.html |title=Filming wraps on The Wolverine, first trailer due early 2013 |work=[[Coventry Telegraph]] |access-date=March 16, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121203053345/http://blogs.coventrytelegraph.net/thegeekfiles/2012/11/filming-wraps-on-the-wolverine-first-trailer-due-early-2013.html |archive-date=December 3, 2012 |url-status=dead}}</ref> and following its July 2013 debut, the Rotten Tomatoes consensus noted that the film "is one superhero movie that manages to stay true to the comics while keeping casual viewers entertained".<ref name="thewolverinert" /> ''The Wolverine'' ended the theatrical run grossing $414 million.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/highest-grossing-movies-2013-2013-12#16-pacific-rim-411-million-1 |title=The 15 Highest-Grossing Movies Of 2013 |last=Acuna |first=Kristen |date=December 24, 2013 |work=[[Business Insider]] |access-date=February 25, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260221131250/https://www.businessinsider.com/highest-grossing-movies-2013-2013-12#16-pacific-rim-411-million-1 |archive-date=February 21, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

==== ''Logan'' (2017) ====
{{Main|Logan (film)|l1=''Logan'' (film)}}

''Logan'' concludes the ''Wolverine'' trilogy and was promoted as Jackman's final performance as Wolverine.<ref name="bfi" /><ref>{{Cite news |title=Logan Review: This Is How Wolverine Was Supposed to End |work=[[Wired (magazine)|Wired]] |date=March 3, 2017 |url=https://www.wired.com/2017/03/logan-review/ |access-date=February 26, 2026 |url-access= |first=Angela |last=Watercutter |archive-date=February 22, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250222212355/https://www.wired.com/2017/03/logan-review/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Mangold once more was tasked with directing.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/fox-sets-dates-wolverine-fantastic-690143 |title=Fox Sets Dates for 'Wolverine,' 'Fantastic Four' Sequels |last=Couch |first=Aaron |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |date=March 20, 2014 |access-date=February 26, 2026 |archive-date=December 25, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161225152128/http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/fox-sets-dates-wolverine-fantastic-690143 |url-status=live}}</ref> He additionally shares screenwriting credit with Frank and [[Michael Green (writer)|Michael Green]], their script depicting a plot derivative of the multi-issue story arc "[[Old Man Logan]]", in which an aged Wolverine defends his mutant daughter [[X-23|Laura]] ([[Dafne Keen]]) from a team of cyborg assassins led by [[Donald Pierce]] ([[Boyd Holbrook]]).<ref>{{Cite news |title=Review: In 'Logan,' a Comic-Book Stalwart Turns Noirish Western |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=March 2, 2017 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/02/movies/logan-review-hugh-jackman-wolverine-x-men.html |access-date=February 26, 2026 |url-access=subscription |first=Manohla |last=Dargis |archive-date=April 16, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170416034249/https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/02/movies/logan-review-hugh-jackman-wolverine-x-men.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |title=The History of the Comic That Inspired Logan and Revolutionized the Marvel Brand |work=[[Vulture (website)|Vulture]] |date=March 1, 2017 |url=https://www.vulture.com/2017/03/the-comic-that-inspired-logan-revolutionized-marvels-x-men.html |access-date=February 26, 2026 |url-access= |first=Abraham |last=Riesman |archive-date=February 15, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260215153110/https://www.vulture.com/2017/03/the-comic-that-inspired-logan-revolutionized-marvels-x-men.html |url-status=live}}</ref> Mangold cited [[Western film]], ''[[Little Miss Sunshine]]'' (2006), and the work of Scottish filmmaker [[Alexander Mackendrick]] as significant influences on ''Logan''{{'s}} creative direction.<ref name="bfi">{{Cite news |title=The films that influenced Logan: director James Mangold on the new Wolverine movie |publisher=[[British Film Institute]] |date=March 1, 2017 |url=https://www.bfi.org.uk/interviews/logan-wolverine-james-mangold-influences |access-date=February 26, 2026 |url-access= |first=Lou |last=Thomas |archive-date=January 17, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260117224515/https://www.bfi.org.uk/interviews/logan-wolverine-james-mangold-influences |url-status=live}}</ref> Fox spent three months on principal photography and released the film to critical acclaim in March 2017.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Where was 'Logan' film? 9 locations to watch for in Hugh Jackman sequel |work=[[The Times-Picayune]] |date=March 1, 2017 |url=https://www.nola.com/entertainment_life/movies_tv/where-was-logan-filmed-9-locations-to-watch-for-in-the-hugh-jackman-sequel/article_5168d602-9eee-587e-8215-59654f5313c6.html |access-date=February 26, 2026 |url-access=subscription |first=Mike |last=Scott |archive-date=February 26, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260226162345/https://www.nola.com/entertainment_life/movies_tv/where-was-logan-filmed-9-locations-to-watch-for-in-the-hugh-jackman-sequel/article_5168d602-9eee-587e-8215-59654f5313c6.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://collider.com/wolverine-3-hugh-jackman-video/ |title=Watch: Hugh Jackman Celebrates the End of 'Wolverine 3' Filming with a Nice Shave |last=Romano |first=Nick |work=[[Collider (website)|Collider]] |date=August 20, 2016 |access-date=February 26, 2026 |archive-date=October 29, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161029065039/http://collider.com/wolverine-3-hugh-jackman-video/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="loganrt" /> ''Logan'' grossed $619.2 million and became the first superhero film to receive an [[Academy Award]] nomination for [[Academy Award for Best Adapted Screenplay|Best Adapted Screenplay]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.boxofficemojo.com/title/tt3315342/?ref_=bo_se_r_1 |title=Logan (2017) |website=[[Box Office Mojo]] |access-date=February 26, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200414135302/https://www.boxofficemojo.com/release/rl3229320705/ |archive-date=April 14, 2020 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Couch |first=Aaron |title='Logan' Breaks Ground with Adapted Screenplay Oscar Nomination |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |date=January 23, 2018 |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/logan-oscar-nominations-film-nominated-best-screenplay-1077336/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240930233619/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/logan-oscar-nominations-film-nominated-best-screenplay-1077336/ |archive-date=September 30, 2024 |access-date=February 26, 2026}}</ref>

=== ''Deadpool'' films ===
==== ''Deadpool'' (2016) ====
{{Main|Deadpool (film)|l1=''Deadpool'' (film)}}
{{anchor|X-Men Origins: Deadpool|Deadpool (2014)}}
[[File:Deadpool 2 Japan Premiere Red Carpet Ryan Reynolds (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright|Ryan Reynolds in 2018]]

''Deadpool'' originated from a joint venture between Marvel and [[Artisan Entertainment]] to adapt no fewer than fifteen Marvel superheroes to film in March 2000.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Fleming |first=Michael |date=May 16, 2000 |title=Artisan deal a real Marvel |url=https://variety.com/2000/film/news/artisan-deal-a-real-marvel-1117781709 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140816044414/http://variety.com/2000/film/news/artisan-deal-a-real-marvel-1117781709/ |archive-date=August 16, 2014 |access-date=October 18, 2014 |work=Variety}}</ref> The partnership continued into [[Lionsgate Films|Lionsgate]]'s takeover of Artisan in 2003, but Lionsgate showed little interest in the contingent of characters they inherited and the film rights soon reverted to Marvel.{{sfnm|1a1=Fritz|1y=2018|1p=54|2a1=Robinson|2a2=Gonzales|2a3=Edwards|2y=2023}} A Deadpool film remained tentatively planned until Fox approved development in May 2009, allocating $50–59 million for the budget.<ref name="reynolds">{{cite news |last=Kit |first=Borys |title='Deadpool' spinoff in works at Fox |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |date=May 5, 2009 |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/deadpool-spinoff-works-fox-83432/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251113154243/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/deadpool-spinoff-works-fox-83432/ |archive-date=November 13, 2025 |url-status=live |access-date=February 26, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Meenan |first=Devin |title=Deadpool Writers Used A Smart Trick To Cut The First Movie's Budget |work=[[SlashFilm|/Film]] |date=September 23, 2024 |url=https://www.slashfilm.com/1655211/deadpool-writers-first-movie-trick-budget-cut/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250720124549/https://www.slashfilm.com/1655211/deadpool-writers-first-movie-trick-budget-cut/ |url-status=live |archive-date=July 20, 2025 |access-date=February 26, 2026}}</ref> It is visual effects specialist [[Tim Miller (director)|Tim Miller]]'s first feature as director, and follows a script written by [[Rhett Reese]] and [[Paul Wernick]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=McClintock |first=Pamela |date=April 8, 2011 |title=Fox Sets Tim Miller to Direct 'Deadpool' |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/fox-sets-tim-miller-direct-176603 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110513175119/http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/fox-sets-tim-miller-direct-176603 |archive-date=May 13, 2011 |access-date=May 9, 2011 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Siegel |first=Tatiana |date=January 5, 2010 |title=Fox taps ''Deadpool'' writers |url=https://variety.com/2010/film/markets-festivals/fox-taps-deadpool-writers-1118013359/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110629174535/http://www.variety.com/article/VR1118013359 |archive-date=June 29, 2011 |access-date=October 23, 2010 |website=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]}}</ref> [[Ryan Reynolds]] stars as [[Wade Wilson (film character)|Deadpool]], reprising the role from ''Wolverine''.<ref name="reynolds" /> Filming began in March 2015 and ended in two months.<ref>{{cite news |last=Levine |first=Nick |title=Ryan Reynolds confirms 'Deadpool' has wrapped filming |work=[[NME]] |date=May 19, 2015 |url=https://www.nme.com/news/film/ryan-reynolds-confirms-deadpool-has-wrapped-filmin-869964 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200808000313/https://www.nme.com/news/film/ryan-reynolds-confirms-deadpool-has-wrapped-filmin-869964 |url-status=live |archive-date=August 8, 2020 |access-date=February 26, 2026}}</ref> Released in February 2016, ''Deadpool'' proved popular critically and commercially, grossing an estimated $783 million, at one point the [[List of highest-grossing R-rated films|highest-grossing R-rated film of all time]].<ref name="deadmt" /><ref>{{cite web |url=https://deadline.com/2017/04/deadpool-box-office-profit-2016-1202057182/ |title=No. 2 'Deadpool' Box Office Profits – 2016 Most Valuable Movie Blockbuster Tournament |last=Fleming |first=Mike Jr. |work=[[Deadline Hollywood]] |date=April 3, 2017 |access-date=February 26, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170609081526/http://deadline.com/2017/04/deadpool-box-office-profit-2016-1202057182/ |archive-date=June 9, 2017 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==== ''Deadpool 2'' (2018) ====
{{Main|Deadpool 2|l1=''Deadpool 2''}}

After months of speculation, Fox ordered ''Deadpool 2'' while ''Deadpool'' ran in theaters in February 2016.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mcnally |first=Victoria |date=September 14, 2015 |title=A 'Deadpool' Sequel Might Already Be In The Works |url=http://www.mtv.com/news/2270191/deadpool-movie-sequel/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161028135859/http://www.mtv.com/news/2270191/deadpool-movie-sequel/ |archive-date=October 28, 2016 |access-date=May 26, 2017 |publisher=MTV}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kit |first=Borys |date=February 9, 2016 |title='Deadpool' Sequel Already in the Works |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/deadpool-sequel-works-863418 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161028135900/http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/deadpool-sequel-works-863418 |archive-date=October 28, 2016 |access-date=February 10, 2016 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref> Reese and Wernick again worked as the screenwriters, developing the script alongside Reynolds and [[Drew Goddard]] as an advisor.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Weintraub |first=Steve |date=February 22, 2017 |title=Exclusive: Drew Goddard Is Working on the 'Deadpool 2' Script [Updated] |url=https://collider.com/deadpool-2-drew-goddard/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170312073406/http://collider.com/deadpool-2-drew-goddard/ |archive-date=March 12, 2017 |access-date=May 26, 2017 |website=[[Collider (website)|Collider]]}}</ref> However, the studio hired a new director, [[David Leitch (filmmaker)|David Leitch]], to replace Miller after he dropped out over creative differences with Reynolds.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kit |first=Borys |date=November 18, 2017 |title=It's Official: 'John Wick' Director David Leitch to Helm 'Deadpool 2' |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/deadpool-2-david-leitch-direct-944728 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161119182914/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/heat-vision/deadpool-2-david-leitch-direct-944728 |archive-date=November 19, 2016 |access-date=May 26, 2017 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref> In the film, Deadpool forms the [[X-Force]] to save an orphaned mutant ([[Julian Dennison]]) from being captured by [[Cable (character)|Cable]] ([[Josh Brolin]]). Filming for ''Deadpool 2'' began in June 2017 and ran until that October.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.dgc.ca/assets/Uploads/BritishColumbia/AvailsProductionLists/Documents/Production-List.pdf |title=DGC BC Production List |publisher=[[Directors Guild of Canada]] |access-date=February 27, 2026 |archive-date=July 25, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170725140234/https://www.dgc.ca/assets/Uploads/BritishColumbia/AvailsProductionLists/Documents/Production-List.pdf |url-status=dead}}</ref> Released in May 2018, the film repeated the success of ''Deadpool'', becoming the year's [[2018 in film#Highest-grossing films|ninth highest-grossing film]] with $785.9 million.<ref name="dead2mt" /><ref name="BOM">{{cite web |url=https://www.boxofficemojo.com/title/tt5463162/ |title=''Deadpool 2'' (2018) |website=[[Box Office Mojo]] |access-date=February 27, 2026 |archive-date=October 25, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191025165929/https://www.boxofficemojo.com/title/tt5463162/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Spin-off ===
==== ''The New Mutants'' (2020) ====
{{Main|The New Mutants (film)|l1=''The New Mutants'' (film)}}
[[File:Anya Taylor-Joy at the 2025 Toronto International Film Festival. 07 (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright|Anya Taylor-Joy in 2025]]

Fox's final ''X-Men'' film, ''The New Mutants'', had been in the works since 2009.<ref name="LaurenDonner">{{Cite web |date=September 22, 2009 |title=Shuler Donner Wants X4 and The New Mutants |url=https://www.comingsoon.net/movies/news/59310-shuler-donner-wants-x4-and-the-new-mutants |access-date=February 27, 2026 |publisher=[[ComingSoon.net]] |archive-date=January 25, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210125001623/https://www.comingsoon.net/movies/news/59310-shuler-donner-wants-x4-and-the-new-mutants |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Josh Boone (director)|Josh Boone]] was chosen to direct and shares scriptwriting credit with his childhood friend Knate Lee.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Fleming |first=Mike Jr. |date=May 13, 2015 |title=The Fault In Our Stars Director To Helm X-Men Spinoff |url=https://deadline.com/2015/05/the-new-mutants-x-men-josh-boone-the-fault-in-our-stars-fox-1201425641/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150514160339/http://deadline.com/2015/05/the-new-mutants-x-men-josh-boone-the-fault-in-our-stars-fox-1201425641/ |archive-date=May 14, 2015 |access-date=May 14, 2015 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref> The duo produced a comic book based on "The Demon Bear Saga" of ''[[The New Mutants (graphic novel)|The New Mutants]]'' (1984) to pitch to Kinberg, which outlined a trilogy of films.<ref name="BooneCS">{{Cite web |last=McKittrick |first=Christopher |date=December 15, 2016 |title=From All We Had to X-Men: Josh Boone, a Busy Man |url=http://creativescreenwriting.com/josh-boone/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170616105323/https://creativescreenwriting.com/josh-boone/ |archive-date=June 16, 2017 |access-date=February 27, 2026 |work=Creative Screenwriting}}</ref><ref name="vulturemutants" /> One goal the filmmakers had was to create a superhero film grounded in [[horror film|horror]].<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Vilkomerson |first=Sara |date=May 25, 2017 |title=''The New Mutants'' details revealed: X-Men franchise goes horror |url=https://ew.com/movies/2017/05/25/the-new-mutants-details-x-men-horror |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170525154243/http://ew.com/movies/2017/05/25/the-new-mutants-details-x-men-horror/ |archive-date=May 25, 2017 |access-date=August 24, 2018 |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]]}}</ref>

Boone found himself at odds with Fox producers over the film's artistic direction; they disliked the script's comedy and treatment of X-Men characters.<ref name="vulturemutants" /> The studio mandated multiple rewrites to bring the screenplay closer to their vision, which Boone rejected.<ref name="vulturemutants" /> ''The New Mutants'' features [[Blu Hunt]], [[Anya Taylor-Joy]], [[Charlie Heaton]], [[Maisie Williams]], and [[Henry Zaga]] as teenage mutants plotting their escape from captivity in a facility run by [[Cecilia Reyes]] ([[Alice Braga]]).<ref>{{Cite news |title='The New Mutants' Review: Maybe All the Cool Super Powers Were Taken |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=August 28, 2020 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/28/movies/the-new-mutants-review.html |access-date=February 27, 2026 |url-access=subscription |first=Amy |last=Nicholson |archive-date=August 7, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220807205018/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/28/movies/the-new-mutants-review.html |url-status=live}}</ref> It was shot over the summer of 2017,<ref name="BostonStory">{{Cite web |last=Slane |first=Kevin |date=August 20, 2020 |title=The story of 'The New Mutants,' the 'cursed' Marvel movie filmed in Mass. |url=https://www.boston.com/culture/entertainment/2020/08/20/new-mutants-movie-filming-location-massachusetts/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200821003032/https://www.boston.com/culture/entertainment/2020/08/20/new-mutants-movie-filming-location-massachusetts |archive-date=August 21, 2020 |access-date=February 27, 2026 |website=[[Boston.com]]}}</ref> but the production was complicated by dissatisfaction with the film's final cut and [[Acquisition of 21st Century Fox by Disney|Fox's acquisition by The Walt Disney Company]].<ref name="vulturemutants">{{Cite web |last=Lee |first=Chris |date=August 26, 2020 |title=What Happened to The New Mutants? |url=https://www.vulture.com/2020/08/what-happened-to-the-new-mutants.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251107030453/https://www.vulture.com/2020/08/what-happened-to-the-new-mutants.html |archive-date=November 7, 2025 |access-date=March 2, 2026 |website=[[Vulture (website)|Vulture]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Rosen |first=Christopher |date=March 9, 2020 |title=The New Mutants Was Delayed for So Long Even Reshoots Weren't Possible |url=https://www.vanityfair.com/hollywood/2020/03/new-mutants-reshoots |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260220172351/https://www.vanityfair.com/hollywood/2020/03/new-mutants-reshoots |archive-date=February 20, 2026 |access-date=March 2, 2026 |website=[[Vanity Fair (magazine)|Vanity Fair]]}}</ref> Delayed three times, ''The New Mutants'' was unsuccessful upon its release in August 2020.<ref name="vulturemutants" />

